  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "proxy-service leaks file descriptors on one error path: connections opened to inventory-api during a request are not closed when that request hits a specific downstream error, so the socket and its fd stay open indefinitely. An earlier spell of inventory-api errors accelerated the leak; open fds climbed from ~8k toward the 40k limit. Once the limit was hit, proxy-service could not accept new connections or open upstream ones ('too many open files'), so ~30% of requests failed. A restart resets the fd count but the leak remains until the error-path code is fixed.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "proxy-service open fds climb steadily toward the 40k limit and do not drop even during low traffic - a leak, not load",
      "the fd growth is tied to un-released connections to inventory-api on an error path",
      "proxy-service ERROR: accept() failing with 'too many open files' (EMFILE)",
      "error_rate rises to ~30% once new connections cannot be opened",
      "restarting the pods resets fds but does not fix the leak"
    ],
    "fix": "Restart affected pods to restore service (done). Fix the inventory-api client to close the connection in a finally block on every path including errors, add an open-fd-count alert, and add a leak test that exercises the error path.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
}
